A||||3||ThatRickGuy||How hard is it?? (pulling engine)||||1||
Z||000000||ThatRickGuy||06-21-2000||04:24 PM||||I wqas talking to my mechanic today, and he was talking about how it was expencive to pull the engine because it was so hard, dealing with lifting the car and what not. So, how can it be so hard? how many bolts hold the cradle to the frame? I know you have to take the top bolt off the strut towers, and hoses, and all, but then you have it out, it would be much easier to pull the engin off the tranny. as aposed to a standard v8 (front engine rear drive) where you have to pull the engine/tranny forward, remove all the trany/bellhousing bolts and deal with angling and what not. if I had a decent way to transport the engine, and some time to do it, I'd be pretty tempted to just try pulling it my self and save the money. ahh well, just wondering what some of you guys who have done this job thought about it.||||reg||1
Z||000001||triker||06-21-2000||04:41 PM||||I spun the #4 rod bearing on my 85SE trike a while back, but 'just happened' to have a 'like new' engine lying around.  I was dreading the swap but did the whole thing by myself, and had it running again in a weekend.  1 day to get the old one out and the new one ready, and 1 day to get the new one in and running.  I don't have AC on my trike, duh, which I'm sure would have slowed me down, but it's no more difficult than any other engine swap. Feel free to e mail me if you want any particulars.||||reg||1
Z||000002||dman||06-21-2000||05:15 PM||||Hi ThatRickGuy,<P>If you do a search on the forum, you'll find lots of references to dropping the cradle. That is one method you can use to remove the engine. Some people have taken the engine out from the top, while others prefer to just drop the cradle; that means the entire drivetrain comes off -- complete engine (with all accessories attached except perhaps the ac compressor), tranny, struts and spindles/wheel hubs.<P>There are only four bolts that hold the cradle in place. Of course there is a whole lot of stuff to disconnect  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">(. You don't need a lift or cherry picker to do it either. Just a couple of good floor jacks and a 4x4, one or two strong furniture dollys will do the trick.<P>Please do a search on the forum, you don't need to look farther than 2 or 3 months for a whole collection of really good posts.<P>I used to have some bookmarks but I've lost them. Search for "4x4" and "cradle" and you'll probably find some good ones.<P>I'd recommend sliding it out from the left or right wheel well instead of sliding it out the back, you won't need to jack it up as high.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||webbee||06-21-2000||07:05 PM||||Hi,<BR>I'm new to this forum so if my tips have been mentioned, sorry.<P>I would say go for it, it's a good afternoon to take it out.<P>I would suggest removing the cv joints/axels first so that you don't have to disturb the rear wheel geometry. This saves a rear wheel alignment. <P>I also over-built a wheeled dolly to drop the cradel onto. My floor jack slides under it, so that once it is on the dolly I can remove the jack and dolly the engine/trans around.<P>I use 2 floor jacks, 1 on a 4x4 to raise/lower the body and the other on the cradel/dolly.<P>I use a heavy duty come-a-long to seperate the engine/transaxel.<P>Haynes/Chilton manuals for reference, and away you go.<P>Good luck and don't be afraid to do it.<P>84 SE 4cylw/auto||||reg||1
